    "ocaml-values-learn": {
      "id": "ocaml-values-learn",
      "title": "Learn: every value has a type",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "ocaml-values"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Describe the types a first OCaml program needs, and what each one holds.",
        "Explain what a type annotation promises the compiler, and what a wrong one does to the build.",
        "Implement a function that turns an integer into its digits, and prove it by running the phase test."
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Read lib/units/units.ml before you write anything. The signature of the function is already there, and the comment above it says what it owes.",
        "The standard library has a function that turns an integer into its digits. Its name says what it does: it makes a string out of an int."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking a value can change its type while the program runs. A value has one type, and the compiler checks every use of it.",
        "Thinking a type annotation is a comment. The compiler reads it, and a wrong one stops the build.",
        "Thinking an integer and a float are the same thing with more digits. They are different types, and the compiler will not mix them for you."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "The OCaml manual: the core library",
          "url": "https://ocaml.org/manual/5.5/core.html"
        },
        {
          "label": "The OCaml tutorial: values and functions",
          "url": "https://ocaml.org/tutorial/values.html"
        }
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Everything in an OCaml program is a value, and every value has a type. An int holds a whole number, a float holds a number with a fraction, a bool holds true or false, a char holds one character, a string holds text, and unit holds nothing at all. A function is a value too, and its type says what it takes and what it gives back."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The compiler checks the type of every expression before the program runs, so a program that has a type error never starts. `let` introduces a name for a value, and the compiler works out the type of the name from the value it is bound to. You can also write the type yourself, and then the compiler checks your word against the code."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The file this phase works in, lib/units/units.ml, with the two functions it holds:",
          "code": "(* The integer as the digits a person reads. *)\nlet show_int (n : int) : string =\n  failwith \"implement show_int\"\n\n(* True when the integer is even. *)\nlet is_even (n : int) : bool =\n  failwith \"implement is_even\""
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The `(n : int)` after the name says the function takes an int, and the `: string` after the parenthesis says it gives a string back. The compiler could have worked both of them out, but a written type is a promise the compiler checks, and a promise in the file is a promise a reader can see."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Two values of the same type are compared with `=`, and `<>` is the same test written the other way round. Both work on ints, floats, chars, bools, strings and tuples of them, and both are a question about values rather than about the program."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "Every value has a type, and the compiler checks it before the program runs.",
            "A function type says what it takes and what it gives back.",
            "An annotation is checked by the compiler, and a wrong one stops the build.",
            "= and <> compare two values of the same type."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "A complete program that keeps the price as cents and prints both parts:",
          "code": "let cents = 199\nlet dollars = cents / 100\nlet remainder = cents mod 100\nlet () = Printf.printf \"%d dollars and %d cents\" dollars remainder"
        },
        {
          "kind": "quickcheck",
          "body": "What does 199 / 100 evaluate to in OCaml?",
          "items": [
            "1, because both numbers are integers.",
            "1.99, because division keeps the decimal part.",
            "2, because the division rounds to the nearest whole number.",
            "An error, because 199 does not divide evenly."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explain": "Both operands are int, so / is integer division and the fractional part is dropped; 199 / 100 is 1."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command that runs the checks for this phase, and the command you run yourself:",
          "code": "dune runtest test/values   # the phase test, by hand\nnode --test --test-reporter=tap test/values.test.mjs   # what the Run button calls"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open lib/units/units.ml and write the body of show_int so that show_int 42 is \"42\" and show_int (-7) is \"-7\". Then run dune runtest test/values and read what it said."
        }
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "state-compare",
        "spec": {
          "before": {
            "title": "without an annotation",
            "lines": [
              "the compiler works",
              "the type out itself"
            ]
          },
          "after": {
            "title": "with an annotation",
            "lines": [
              "show_int : int -> string",
              "the compiler checks",
              "your word against it"
            ]
          }
        },
        "caption": "A written type is a promise the compiler checks. It could have worked the type out, but a promise in the file is a promise a reader can see."
      }
    },
