    "ocaml-lists-learn": {
      "id": "ocaml-lists-learn",
      "title": "Learn: a list is a value",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "ocaml-lists"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Describe how a list is built, and what the empty list is.",
        "Explain why a function that walks a list calls itself, and what the rec keyword says to the compiler.",
        "Implement a function that adds up a list of numbers, and prove it by running the phase test."
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Read lib/series/series.ml before you write anything. The two functions walk a list, and one of them builds a list while it walks.",
        "A list is either empty or a head with a tail. The empty list is written with two brackets, and the head is joined to the tail with a colon."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking a list holds its elements in a block, so the first one is at the front of memory. A list is built out of pairs, and the first one is simply the one you can reach first.",
        "Thinking a list has to be walked with an index. There is no index in a list, so a function walks it by matching on its shape.",
        "Thinking the rec keyword is a matter of style. It tells the compiler that the function calls itself, and without it the call has nothing to refer to."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "The OCaml manual: the core library",
          "url": "https://ocaml.org/manual/5.5/core.html"
        },
        {
          "label": "The OCaml tutorial: lists",
          "url": "https://ocaml.org/tutorial/lists.html"
        }
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A list in OCaml is a value, and it is either empty or a head joined to a tail. The empty list is written `[]`, and a list of three numbers is written `[1; 2; 3]`. The same list can be written as a head and a tail, `1 :: [2; 3]`, and the two are the same value."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A list is taken apart by matching on it. The pattern `[]` matches the empty list, and the pattern `x :: rest` matches a list with a head `x` and a tail `rest`. A function that walks a list is written `let rec`, because it calls itself on the tail. The `rec` is what tells the compiler that the name refers to the function being defined."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The file this phase works in, lib/series/series.ml:",
          "code": "(* Every number in the list, added up. *)\nlet rec sum (xs : int list) : int =\n  failwith \"implement sum\"\n\n(* The same list, with every number doubled. *)\nlet rec double_all (xs : int list) : int list =\n  failwith \"implement double_all\""
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A function that walks a list has two cases and no more: what to do when the list is empty, and what to do when it has a head."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The empty case is where the walk ends, and it is where the answer that does not need the rest of the list is written. The other case does one piece of work and then calls the function on the tail."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "A list is either empty or a head joined to a tail.",
            "x :: rest joins one element to a list.",
            "A list is taken apart by matching on its shape.",
            "A function that calls itself is written let rec."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "A complete walk that builds a new list, one head at a time:",
          "code": "let rec double_all (xs : int list) : int list =\n  match xs with\n  | [] -> []\n  | x :: rest -> (x * 2) :: double_all rest\n\nlet () = List.iter (fun n -> Printf.printf \"%d \" n) (double_all [1; 2; 3])"
        },
        {
          "kind": "quickcheck",
          "body": "What is double_all [1; 2; 3]?",
          "items": [
            "[2; 4; 6], because each head is doubled.",
            "[1; 2; 3], because the list is unchanged.",
            "6, because the numbers are added together.",
            "An error, because the function is not tail recursive."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explain": "The pattern x :: rest doubles the head and conses it onto the doubled tail, so the order is kept."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command that runs the checks for this phase, and the command you run yourself:",
          "code": "dune runtest test/lists   # the phase test, by hand\nnode --test --test-reporter=tap test/lists.test.mjs   # what the Run button calls"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open lib/series/series.ml and write the body of sum so that it adds up every number in the list, and so that the empty list adds up to zero. Then run dune runtest test/lists and read what it said."
        }
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "event-flow",
        "spec": {
          "steps": [
            "sum [1; 2; 3]",
            "sum [2; 3]",
            "sum [3]",
            "sum []",
            "sum [] is 0",
            "3, 5, then 6"
          ],
          "moments": [
            {
              "step": 0,
              "note": "The first call needs the sum of the tail, so it waits."
            },
            {
              "step": 1,
              "note": "The same function is called again with one fewer number."
            },
            {
              "step": 2,
              "note": "The walk reaches a list with one number."
            },
            {
              "step": 3,
              "note": "The empty list is the case that does not call itself."
            },
            {
              "step": 4,
              "note": "The empty list answers 0, and the calls start to return."
            },
            {
              "step": 5,
              "note": "Each waiting call adds its head: 3, then 5, then 6."
            }
          ]
        },
        "caption": "The call on the tail waits for the answer, so the walk goes down to the empty list and the additions come back up: 3, then 5, then 6."
      },
      "bridge": {
        "course": "task-manager",
        "phase": "fundamentals",
        "text": "In JavaScript an array keeps its elements in order and you count from zero. In OCaml a list is either empty or a head joined to a tail, so taking one apart is a match on its shape."
      }
    },
