    "ocaml-records-learn": {
      "id": "ocaml-records-learn",
      "title": "Learn: a value with named fields",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "ocaml-records"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Describe what a record is, and how its type is declared.",
        "Explain why changing a field gives back a new record and leaves the old one alone.",
        "Implement a function that reads a field of a record, and prove it by running the phase test."
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Read lib/account/account.ml before you write anything. The type of the record is declared at the top of the file, and the two functions work on it.",
        "A field of a record is read with a dot, and the name of the record goes in front of it."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking a record is a block of memory with a shape, so that changing a field changes the record. A record is a value, and a value cannot change.",
        "Thinking the type declaration is optional. A record without a declared type has fields the compiler cannot check.",
        "Thinking the record update syntax changes the record it was given. It builds a new one and leaves the old one as it was."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "The OCaml manual: the core language",
          "url": "https://ocaml.org/manual/5.5/core.html"
        },
        {
          "label": "The OCaml tutorial: data types and pattern matching",
          "url": "https://ocaml.org/tutorial/datatypes.html"
        }
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A record is a value with named fields. The type is declared with `type`, the name of the record, an equals sign, and the fields between braces, each with its own type and separated by semicolons. A record of that type is written with the same braces, and the compiler checks every field against the declaration."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The file this phase works in, lib/account/account.ml:",
          "code": "type account = { name : string; cents : int }\n\n(* The amount the account holds. *)\nlet balance (a : account) : int =\n  failwith \"implement balance\"\n\n(* The same account, with the amount added to it. *)\nlet deposit (a : account) (amount : int) : account =\n  failwith \"implement deposit\""
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A field is read with a dot: `a.cents` is the cents of the account `a`. A record is a value, and a value never changes, so there is no way to set a field. What there is instead is the record update, written `{ a with cents = ... }`, which builds a new record from the old one with one field replaced and the rest carried over."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "That is why a function that changes something has to give the new record back. The caller decides what to do with it, and the old record is still there, untouched, for anyone who is still holding it."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "A record is a value with named fields, and the type is declared.",
            "a.cents reads a field.",
            "A value never changes, so a field is never set.",
            "{ a with field = value } builds a new record and leaves the old one alone."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "A complete deposit that returns a new record and leaves the old one alone:",
          "code": "type account = { name : string; cents : int }\n\nlet balance (a : account) : int = a.cents\n\nlet deposit (a : account) (amount : int) : account =\n  { a with cents = a.cents + amount }\n\nlet () =\n  let a = { name = \"Ada\"; cents = 500 } in\n  let b = deposit a 250 in\n  Printf.printf \"%d %d\" (balance a) (balance b)"
        },
        {
          "kind": "quickcheck",
          "body": "What does balance a read after let b = deposit a 250?",
          "items": [
            "500, because a was not changed.",
            "750, because deposit writes the new amount.",
            "250, because the function returns the amount.",
            "An error, because records cannot be read after an update."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explain": "The update builds b from a with one field replaced; a still holds its original cents."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command that runs the checks for this phase, and the command you run yourself:",
          "code": "dune runtest test/records   # the phase test, by hand\nnode --test --test-reporter=tap test/records.test.mjs   # what the Run button calls"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open lib/account/account.ml and write the body of balance so that it gives back the amount the account holds. Then run dune runtest test/records and read what it said."
        }
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "state-compare",
        "spec": {
          "before": {
            "title": "the old record",
            "lines": [
              "name = Ada",
              "cents = 199"
            ]
          },
          "after": {
            "title": "the new record",
            "lines": [
              "name = Ada",
              "cents = 250"
            ]
          }
        },
        "caption": "A value never changes. The update builds a new record with one field replaced and the rest carried over, and the old record is still there for anyone holding it."
      },
      "bridge": {
        "course": "task-manager",
        "phase": "fundamentals",
        "text": "In JavaScript an object holds named fields and you find one by its key. In OCaml a record has its fields and their types written down, and a value never changes, so a changed field means a new record."
      }
    },
