    "ocaml-records-ask": {
      "id": "ocaml-records-ask",
      "title": "Reason: what a record cannot do",
      "kind": "question",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "review",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "ocaml-records"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Ask what a record is, and what every value in the language is."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking a record is a block of memory that can be written to.",
        "Thinking the update syntax changes the record it names."
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A program wants to add fifty to the balance of an account it was given. What does OCaml let it do, and what does it have to do instead?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        "Write to the field of the account it was given, and the account now holds the new amount.",
        "Build a new account from the old one with the balance replaced, and give that new account back.",
        "Ask the record to change itself, and the record refuses if nobody is holding it any more.",
        "Nothing can be done with an account, because a record is a fixed shape and its fields cannot hold new values."
      ],
      "answer": 1,
      "explanation": "Every value in OCaml is immutable, so no record field is written to. The record update builds a new record with the field replaced and the others carried over; that is what comes back. That is not a limitation dressed up: two functions can share a record, neither changes what the other sees, and it refuses nothing. It is not a fixed shape that cannot hold new values."
    },
