    "ocaml-variants-learn": {
      "id": "ocaml-variants-learn",
      "title": "Learn: a value that is one of several shapes",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "ocaml-variants"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Describe what a variant type is, and what each of its constructors carries.",
        "Explain what the compiler checks about a match over a variant, and what it reports when a case is missing.",
        "Implement a function that writes an expression as text by matching on its shape."
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Read lib/ast/ast.ml before you write anything. The type is declared at the top of the file, and the two functions below it both match on it.",
        "Each constructor of the type is one shape of an expression. A match on an expression asks which shape it is, and each branch knows what that shape carries."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking a variant is a record with a tag. The constructors are names, and each one decides what the value holds.",
        "Thinking a match over a variant is a chain of conditions in another spelling. The compiler checks the match against the constructors, and it reports a constructor nobody handled.",
        "Thinking the pattern `_` makes the match exhaustive. It matches any value, so the compiler says nothing about the constructors you left out."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "The OCaml manual: the core language",
          "url": "https://ocaml.org/manual/5.5/core.html"
        },
        {
          "label": "The OCaml tutorial: data types and pattern matching",
          "url": "https://ocaml.org/tutorial/datatypes.html"
        }
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A variant type is a value that is one of several shapes. The type is declared with `type`, and each shape is a constructor: a name, and what the value of that shape holds. `Num of float` is a shape that holds a number, `Add of expr * expr` is a shape that holds two expressions, and `Neg of expr` holds one."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The type this phase is about, declared at the top of lib/ast/ast.ml:",
          "code": "type expr =\n  | Num of float\n  | Add of expr * expr\n  | Mul of expr * expr\n  | Div of expr * expr\n  | Neg of expr"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The bar in front of a constructor is the same bar that starts a branch of a match, and it means the same thing: this branch is about that shape."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A match over a variant is checked by the compiler against the constructors of the type, so a constructor nobody handled is reported while the code is compiled. That is the thing a chain of conditions cannot do: there is no way for the compiler to know that a value of a new shape has slipped through."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A constructor is written with its name alone to make a value, so `Num 2.5` is the number two and a half in the shape Num. In a match, the constructor is a pattern, and the pattern can name what it holds: `Num n` binds the number to `n`, and `Add (a, b)` binds the two expressions to `a` and `b`."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "A variant type is a value that is one of several shapes.",
            "Each constructor names a shape and says what that shape holds.",
            "A match on a variant is checked against the constructors.",
            "A constructor pattern can name what it holds."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "A complete show with one branch for every constructor:",
          "code": "type expr =\n  | Num of float\n  | Add of expr * expr\n  | Mul of expr * expr\n  | Div of expr * expr\n  | Neg of expr\n\nlet rec show (e : expr) : string =\n  match e with\n  | Num n -> string_of_float n\n  | Add (a, b) -> \"(\" ^ show a ^ \" + \" ^ show b ^ \")\"\n  | Mul (a, b) -> \"(\" ^ show a ^ \" * \" ^ show b ^ \")\"\n  | Div (a, b) -> \"(\" ^ show a ^ \" / \" ^ show b ^ \")\"\n  | Neg a -> \"-\" ^ show a\n\nlet () = print_string (show (Add (Num 1.0, Neg (Num 2.0))))"
        },
        {
          "kind": "quickcheck",
          "body": "What does the compiler do if a match over expr misses Neg?",
          "items": [
            "It warns that the match is not exhaustive.",
            "It adds the missing branch automatically at run time.",
            "It refuses to compile the type declaration.",
            "It treats the missing constructor as a wildcard."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explain": "The compiler knows every constructor, so a missing one makes the match non-exhaustive and produces a warning; _ would silence it."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command that runs the checks for this phase, and the command you run yourself:",
          "code": "dune runtest test/variants   # the phase test, by hand\nnode --test --test-reporter=tap test/variants.test.mjs   # what the Run button calls"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open lib/ast/ast.ml and write the body of show so that it writes an expression as the text a person reads: a number as its digits, and an addition with both sides inside brackets. Then run dune runtest test/variants and read what it said."
        }
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "pattern-match",
        "spec": {
          "value": "Add (Num 2.5, Num 4.0)",
          "cases": [
            {
              "pattern": "Num n",
              "binds": "n is 2.5",
              "note": "a shape that holds a number"
            },
            {
              "pattern": "Add (a, b)",
              "binds": "a and b are the two sides",
              "note": "a shape that holds two expressions"
            }
          ],
          "fired": 1
        },
        "caption": "The compiler checks a match against the constructors of the type, so a constructor nobody handled is reported while the code is compiled."
      }
    },
