    "ocaml-modules-learn": {
      "id": "ocaml-modules-learn",
      "title": "Learn: modules, signatures and functors",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "ocaml-modules"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Explain how another module reaches a module by its name, and what a signature adds to that.",
        "Describe what a functor is given, and what the module it builds needs from it.",
        "Implement an evaluator that matches over the expression type, and prove it by running the phase test."
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Read lib/eval/eval.ml and lib/eval/eval.mli before you write anything. One of them is the code and the other is the promise.",
        "The expression type is in the library of the phase before this one, so the module name is part of the name you write."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking a module is a namespace that only holds names. A module holds values and functions, and it is compiled into one object file like anything else.",
        "Thinking a signature file is a comment file. The compiler builds the module against it, and anything the signature does not name cannot be reached from outside.",
        "Thinking a functor is a function. A functor is a module built from a module, and what it is given is a whole module with its own types and functions."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "The OCaml manual: the module system",
          "url": "https://ocaml.org/manual/5.5/modules.html"
        },
        {
          "label": "The OCaml tutorial: modules",
          "url": "https://ocaml.org/tutorial/modules.html"
        }
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A module in OCaml is a file, and its name is the name of the file. A library is a group of modules, and the dune file of the library says which modules are in it."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Another module reaches one by its name: `Eval.eval` is the function eval of the module Eval, and the compiler checks that the module Eval is one of the libraries this module depends on."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A signature is a second file beside the module, with the same name and the extension mli. It says what the module promises: the types and the values that other code may use. The compiler builds the module against the signature, so anything the signature does not name cannot be reached from outside, and the promise can be read without reading the code."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The module this phase writes, and the signature that says what it promises:",
          "code": "(* The evaluator: the value of an expression, computed from its shape. *)\n\n(* The value of the expression. A division by zero raises Division_by_zero. *)\nlet rec eval (e : Ast.expr) : float =\n  failwith \"implement eval\""
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "And the promise, in lib/eval/eval.mli:",
          "code": "(* The evaluator's promise: one function, and nothing else. *)\n\nval eval : Ast.expr -> float"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A functor is a module built from another module. It is written `module Make_thing (C : COMPARABLE) = struct ... end`, and the module type COMPARABLE says what the module it is given has to provide. The body of the functor uses that module as if it were written there, and the functor is applied to a module to make a module of its own."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The module type the counter in lib/table/table.ml asks for:",
          "code": "module type COMPARABLE = sig\n  type t\n  val compare : t -> t -> int\n  val show : t -> string\nend"
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "A module is a file, and other code reaches it by its name.",
            "A signature is what a module promises, and it hides the rest.",
            "A functor is a module built from a module.",
            "A module type says what the module a functor is given has to provide."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "A complete signature, a module that fits it, and the module the functor builds:",
          "code": "module type COMPARABLE = sig\n  type t\n  val compare : t -> t -> int\n  val show : t -> string\nend\n\nmodule Int_key : COMPARABLE = struct\n  type t = int\n  let compare (a : int) (b : int) : int = a - b\n  let show (n : int) : string = string_of_int n\nend\n\nmodule Ordered (C : COMPARABLE) = struct\n  let same (a : C.t) (b : C.t) : bool = C.compare a b = 0\n  let label (k : C.t) : string = C.show k\nend\n\nmodule Int_ordered = Ordered (Int_key)\n\nlet () = Printf.printf \"%b %s\" (Int_ordered.same 2 2) (Int_ordered.label 7)"
        },
        {
          "kind": "quickcheck",
          "body": "What does Ordered (Int_key) produce?",
          "items": [
            "A module whose functions use Int_key.",
            "A function that compares two integers immediately.",
            "A new signature that replaces COMPARABLE.",
            "A value of type int."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explain": "A functor takes a module and returns a module; Ordered (Int_key) builds one whose functions call the Int_key it was given."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command that runs the checks for this phase, and the command you run yourself:",
          "code": "dune runtest test/modules   # the phase test, by hand\nnode --test --test-reporter=tap test/modules.test.mjs   # what the Run button calls"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open lib/eval/eval.ml and write the body of eval so that it gives back the value of an expression, and so that a division by zero raises the error the library provides. Then run dune runtest test/modules and read what it said."
        }
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "event-flow",
        "spec": {
          "steps": [
            "SERIES signature",
            "Series : SERIES",
            "Make(Series)",
            "compare and show"
          ],
          "moments": [
            {
              "step": 0,
              "note": "The signature says what a module must provide, not how it does it."
            },
            {
              "step": 1,
              "note": "Series provides compare and show, so it fits the signature."
            },
            {
              "step": 2,
              "note": "The functor Make takes Series and builds a module from it."
            },
            {
              "step": 3,
              "note": "The built module uses compare and show from the module it was given."
            }
          ]
        },
        "caption": "A functor is a module built from another module. Make(Series) takes the functions Series promises and builds the counter with them."
      }
    },
