    "ocaml-stdlib-learn": {
      "id": "ocaml-stdlib-learn",
      "title": "Learn: the library you already have",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "ocaml-stdlib"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Describe what the standard library holds, and why reading it is part of learning the language.",
        "Explain what an option is, and what it lets the caller do with a value that may not be there.",
        "Implement a function that turns a result into a line of text, and prove it by running the phase test."
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Read lib/report/report.ml before you write anything. The two functions are about the two library types the calculator leans on: a result and an option.",
        "The library has a function that turns a float into its digits, and a function that joins a list of strings with a separator."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking the standard library is something to avoid. It is the code most OCaml programs are made of, and writing a list fold again is work nobody thanks you for.",
        "Thinking an option is the same as a result. A result says whether a computation worked, and an option says whether there is a value at all.",
        "Thinking the library functions are magic. Each one is a function like any other, and the manual says what it does and what it does to an empty list."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "The OCaml manual: the standard library",
          "url": "https://ocaml.org/manual/5.5/stdlib.html"
        },
        {
          "label": "The OCaml standard library API",
          "url": "https://ocaml.org/api/5.5/index.html"
        }
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The standard library is the code that comes with the compiler, and it is large: lists, strings, maps, sets, buffers, the printers, the file system and more. A good part of learning OCaml is learning what is already there, because the library is written in OCaml and reads like OCaml."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Three of its types come up in this project. A list is a value that is empty or a head and a tail. A result is a value that is an Ok or an Error."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "An option is a value that is a Some holding a value or a None holding nothing, and it is what a function gives back when there may be no answer: the average of no numbers is None rather than a number that means nothing."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The file this phase works in, lib/report/report.ml:",
          "code": "(* The answer as a line of text. *)\nlet describe (r : (float, string) result) : string =\n  failwith \"implement describe\"\n\n(* The mean of the numbers, when there are any. *)\nlet average (xs : float list) : float option =\n  failwith \"implement average\""
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The list functions are the ones that come up most. `List.map` applies a function to every element, `List.fold_left` walks a list and folds it into one value, and `List.length` counts the elements. Each one says what it does with an empty list, and that is written down in the manual."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "The standard library is the code most OCaml programs are made of.",
            "List.map, List.fold_left and List.length are the ones to reach for.",
            "An option is Some or None: a value that may not be there.",
            "A fold walks a list and turns it into one value."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "A complete describe and average, using the list functions the phase names:",
          "code": "let describe (r : (float, string) result) : string =\n  match r with\n  | Ok value -> Printf.sprintf \"answer: %.2f\" value\n  | Error reason -> \"no answer: \" ^ reason\n\nlet average (xs : float list) : float option =\n  match xs with\n  | [] -> None\n  | _ -> Some (List.fold_left ( +. ) 0.0 xs /. float_of_int (List.length xs))\n\nlet () = print_endline (describe (Ok 2.5))"
        },
        {
          "kind": "quickcheck",
          "body": "What is List.fold_left ( + ) 0 [1; 2; 3]?",
          "items": [
            "6, because it adds every element to 0.",
            "3, because the last element is the answer.",
            "[1; 2; 3], because fold keeps the list.",
            "0, because the accumulator is returned unchanged."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explain": "fold_left carries an accumulator from the left; 0 + 1 + 2 + 3 is 6."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command that runs the checks for this phase, and the command you run yourself:",
          "code": "dune runtest test/stdlib   # the phase test, by hand\nnode --test --test-reporter=tap test/stdlib.test.mjs   # what the Run button calls"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open lib/report/report.ml and write the body of describe so that it turns an answer into a line of text: the value with its label, or the reason with its label. Then run dune runtest test/stdlib and read what it said."
        }
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "pattern-match",
        "spec": {
          "value": "None",
          "cases": [
            {
              "pattern": "Some x",
              "binds": "x is the value",
              "note": "there is an answer"
            },
            {
              "pattern": "None",
              "binds": "nothing is bound",
              "note": "there is no answer"
            }
          ],
          "fired": 1
        },
        "caption": "An option is the shape of an answer that may not be there. The average of no numbers is None, not a number that means nothing."
      }
    },
